`context/instructions/memo.md` is the contract for planned operations that have
no host scheduler here. Read it when `context/memo.yaml` is relevant: a session
start, a due listing, or a request to add or record an operation.

## The register

`context/memo.yaml` is the planned-operation register: recurring **rules** and
one-off **memos**. It is **CLI-owned** — read it, and change it only through
`sdt context memo` (see `context/instructions/cli.md`), never by hand.

- A **rule** is an operation with a cadence (`every_days`) and the day it was
  last done (`last_done`). It is due when `last_done + every_days <= today`; a
  rule with no `last_done` is due now.
- A **memo** is a one-off reminder with a `due` date. It is due when
  `today >= due` and it is not done.

Both carry an optional `action` (the project's command or hint) and `source`
(where the operation is documented).

## The due check

At session start, run `sdt context memo due`; it reads the system date. When
nothing is due it prints nothing and costs no context. This check is the only
trigger: SDT has no scheduler, hook or daemon and cannot enforce it, so a
missed check is possible and visible only in the register.

## Acting on a due item

- **Propose, never block or run silently.** Tell the user what is due and ask
  whether to run it before or after the work they requested. Never refuse the
  requested task because something is overdue.
- **One operation per run, bounded scope.** A due dependency update is not a
  general refactor. The project owns the safety rules — what may run
  automatically, what is only reported (for example majors), on which branch,
  and what to test — and the register's `action`/`source` point at them.
- **Record `done` only after success.** `sdt context memo done <id>` sets
  `last_done` (a rule) or `done` (a memo). Call it only after the operation
  succeeded: SDT cannot verify it, and a failed run must not update the
  register.

## Boundaries

- The check reads recorded state and emits a signal; it never mutates the
  register, never fixes anything and never enforces.
- Adding an operation means a rule or a memo in the register, not a new
  document kind or lifecycle stage.
